{"answer":"The monthly count of the communal reserve will be done by the rotating steward each month’s first Saturday. They will open the locked chest, weigh the contents with a communal scale, and write the total in the ledger. If the reserve turns out to be lower than the amount we expected (for example, if we were supposed to have 30 kg but only 20 kg is there), we’ll call a quick meeting of all fishers. We’ll then agree to either reduce the 10 kg limit for the next month, or bring in extra fish from nearby lakes to top the reserve back up. The goal is to keep the reserve high enough that no one has to sit out a trip because they took too much in the past.

I’ve kept my proposal the same:
No fisher may take more than 10 kg per trip. At each trip’s return, each fisher must weigh and record their haul on a communal ledger. Any catch above 10 kg must be set aside into a communal reserve equal to the excess weight. A rotating steward will check the ledger daily and enforce that no one exceeds the limit; if a fisher exceeds, they must either return the excess weight or sit out the next trip as a penalty. The ledger will be kept on the community’s common shelf, with each fisher marking their weight and the date. The rotating steward will review the ledger every morning. Excess weight will be deposited into a locked communal reserve chest that only the steward can access. Requests for reserve use must be written, signed by the fisher, and approved by the steward. The reserve will be counted monthly to ensure it remains sustainable.","revised_policy":"No fisher may take more than 10 kg per trip. At each trip’s return, each fisher must weigh and record their haul on a communal ledger. Any catch above 10 kg must be set aside into a communal reserve equal to the excess weight. A rotating steward will check the ledger daily and enforce that no one exceeds the limit; if a fisher exceeds, they must either return the excess weight or sit out the next trip as a penalty. The ledger will be kept on the community’s common shelf, with each fisher marking their weight and the date. The rotating steward will review the ledger every morning. Excess weight will be deposited into a locked communal reserve chest that only the steward can access. Requests for reserve use must be written, signed by the fisher, and approved by the steward. The reserve will be counted monthly by the steward to ensure it remains sustainable.","revised_operationalization":"The reserve will be weighed by the rotating steward on the first Saturday of each month, the total recorded in the ledger, and discussed in a community meeting if it falls short. If the reserve is insufficient, the community will agree on a short‑term quota adjustment or arrange to bring in extra fish to replenish it.","reasoning":"I answered as Beti, focused on my own and the community’s well‑being, and kept the policy unchanged because the question didn’t require a change."}

{"answer":"I think the community will feel more secure if we put a clear line on this.  I suggest that a fisher who records a catch over 3 kg on three separate trips, not necessarily in a row but within any span of five trips, will be given a two‑trip ban.  The rotating monitor who reviews the ledger on that trip will be the one to announce and enforce the ban, with the ban logged in the community ledger so everyone knows it.  This keeps the rule simple and lets us keep fishing in a sustainable way while still protecting the lake.

"No individual fisher may take more than 3 kg per trip. All fishers must record their catch in a shared ledger at the end of each trip. The community will collectively monitor compliance. If a fisher exceeds 3 kg, the excess fish must be returned to the lake and a fine equal to the value of the excess (excess weight × prevailing market price per kg for that species) is paid to the community fund. The community fund is kept in a shared chest overseen by the rotating monitors. Repeated violations trigger a temporary fishing ban.  A ban is imposed after three violations within any five‑trip window, lasts two trips, and is decided by the rotating monitor who reviews the ledger. The rule applies to every trip until the lake stock is restored."

